.TH FACELOCK 1 "2026-08-12" "facelock" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
facelock \- Linux face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B facelock
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B facelock
is a unified command-line tool for Linux face authentication. It handles
face enrollment, authentication, daemon management, camera preview,
benchmarking, and system setup. The companion PAM module
.BR pam_facelock (8)
connects to the facelock daemon or spawns
.B facelock auth
to perform face-based authentication for system services such as
.BR sudo (8)
and login managers.
.PP
All face recognition inference runs locally using ONNX models (SCRFD for
detection and ArcFace for embedding). No cloud services are contacted.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These are declared once and accepted by every subcommand, on either side of the
subcommand name \(em \fBfacelock \-c \fIpath\fB daemon\fR and
\fBfacelock daemon \-c \fIpath\fR are the same invocation.
.TP
.B \-c\fR, \fB\-\-config \fIpath\fR
Path to the config file. Takes precedence over
.BR FACELOCK_CONFIG .
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress informational stdout; errors, prompts and exit codes are unchanged.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Download models and create directories. An interactive wizard guides initial
configuration.
.IP
Three rules govern the flags below. Supplying a value answers that question and
therefore replaces the corresponding prompt \(em there is no
\fB\-\-skip\-\fIx\fB\-prompt\fR family, because on the steps that change the
system "skip" would have to mean "apply the default", which is the opposite of
what an integrator asking to skip PAM wants. A \fB\-\-no\-\fIaction\fR flag
declines an action outright; declining is not the same as defaulting. And
\fBauto\fR means re-derive from the hardware, not "use the default" \(em
omitting the flag already gives the default.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-non\-interactive
Run in non-interactive mode (skip wizard). Performs the base setup only:
directories, model download and verification, encryption, group membership and
path permissions. No PAM, no systemd, and no enrollment unless explicitly
requested.
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip confirmation prompts, and unlock the gate that otherwise refuses to modify
a sensitive PAM service file (\fBcommon\-auth\fR, \fBlogin\fR,
\fBpassword\-auth\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\-ac\fR, \fBsshd\fR,
\fBsystem\-auth\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\-ac\fR, \fBsystem\-login\fR).
\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR suppresses the per-file confirmation on its own but
does \fInot\fR unlock that gate. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-pam
Install or manage PAM module configuration. On its own, configures one service
(see \fB\-\-service\fR) and touches nothing else.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pam
Decline PAM configuration entirely. No file under
.I /etc/pam.d
is read, backed up or written.
.TP
.B \-\-systemd
Install and enable systemd units for the facelock daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-systemd
Decline systemd configuration. No unit files are written and
.BR systemctl (1)
is not invoked.
.TP
.B \-\-enroll
Enroll a face during setup, without the confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-enroll
Do not enroll a face during setup. The recognition test that depends on it is
skipped as well.
.TP
.B \-\-disable
Used with \fB\-\-systemd\fR: disable and stop systemd units instead. Requires
\fB\-\-systemd\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-service \fIname\fR
Used with \fB\-\-pam\fR: target PAM service (default: sudo). Requires
\fB\-\-pam\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Used with \fB\-\-pam\fR: remove the facelock PAM line instead of adding it.
Requires \fB\-\-pam\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-if\-present
Used with \fB\-\-pam \-\-remove\fR: treat an absent service file as success
rather than an error. Read, parse and write failures remain fatal. Requires
\fB\-\-remove\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-camera \fIpath\fR|\fBauto\fR
Camera device path, or \fBauto\fR to re-detect the IR camera from the attached
hardware. \fBauto\fR fails if zero or more than one IR-capable device is found;
an explicit path that does not exist is also an error.
.TP
.B \-\-models \fBstandard\fR|\fBbalanced\fR|\fBhigh\fR
Model quality preset. Selects the detector/embedder pair to configure and
download.
.TP
.B \-\-execution\-provider \fBcpu\fR|\fBcuda\fR|\fBrocm\fR|\fBopenvino\fR|\fBauto\fR
ONNX Runtime execution provider. \fBauto\fR asks the installed ONNX Runtime
which providers it was built with and selects cuda, rocm, openvino or cpu in
that order of preference, printing what it found. Availability is a property of
the ONNX Runtime build, not of the hardware: a CPU-only runtime reports no GPU
providers even on a machine with a GPU. If the runtime cannot be queried at
all, \fBauto\fR warns and selects cpu rather than failing the setup.
.TP
.B \-\-encryption \fBtpm\fR|\fBkeyfile\fR|\fBnone\fR|\fBauto\fR
Embedding encryption method. \fBauto\fR uses the TPM when a working TPM 2.0 is
present and a software keyfile otherwise. \fBtpm\fR fails rather than falling
back.
.RE
.IP
Flags compose. \fB\-\-pam\fR and/or \fB\-\-systemd\fR on their own perform just
that action; any flag that only makes sense while the base setup runs
(\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR, a choice flag, or any of \fB\-\-no\-pam\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-systemd\fR, \fB\-\-enroll\fR, \fB\-\-no\-enroll\fR) forces the base
setup, and the requested actions run in addition to it. For an action pair, the
later flag on the command line wins.
.IP
\fB\-\-pam\fR is an alias onto
.B "facelock pam add"
and
.BR "facelock pam remove" ,
which is the primary spelling and the one that takes several services in one
process. Every \fBsetup \-\-pam\fR invocation keeps parsing and keeps its
behaviour, including the rule that \fB\-y\fR is what unlocks a sensitive
service here where \fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR does it on
.BR "facelock pam add" .
.TP
.B is\-enrolled
Report whether a user has a usable face enrollment. The exit status is the
contract: 0 if enrolled, 1 if not, 2 on error; stdout is the state word
.B enrolled
or
.BR not\-enrolled ,
after the fashion of
.BR "systemctl is\-active" .
See
.B EXIT STATUS
below. It reads only the per-user marker file under
.IR /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/ ;
it does not activate the daemon over D-Bus, does not open a camera, and does
not read the face database. It never errors merely because the caller is
outside the
.B facelock
group, but it cannot report
.B enrolled
for one either: the marker sits under two 0710 root:facelock directories, so a
non-member reads EACCES and is reported
.BR not\-enrolled .
That is the correct answer \(em the group is required to reach the daemon at
all, so face auth genuinely is not operational for that caller yet. The marker
is a hint for deciding whether to offer a face-auth affordance \(em PAM at
authentication time remains authoritative.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output a JSON object with the keys \fBenrolled\fR (boolean), \fBmodels\fR
(integer) and \fBupdated\fR (ISO 8601 timestamp, or null) instead of the state
word. \fBmodels\fR is 0 and \fBupdated\fR is null when the user is not
enrolled. Does not change the exit status.
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress stdout and report only via the exit status. Errors are still written
to stderr. This is the global flag (see GLOBAL OPTIONS), so
\fBfacelock \-\-quiet is\-enrolled\fR is the same invocation.
.RE
.TP
.B capabilities
Report what this build can do, as capability names \(em one per line on stdout.
Answers from the binary's own clap tree and compiled-in constants: it reads no
config file, activates no daemon and opens no camera, and so needs no root. It
is what replaces grepping \fB\-\-help\fR in a wrapper script, since help text
is not an API. Probe by name, never by version: a distro or git build can carry
a version that says nothing about what is in it.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit one JSON object with the keys \fBversion\fR (this binary's own version)
and \fBcapabilities\fR (a sorted, deduplicated array of names) instead of the
plain list.
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress stdout, leaving the exit status as the whole answer. This is the
global flag (see GLOBAL OPTIONS).
.RE
.IP
Both forms exit 0; the command has no failure mode. A build that predates the
command answers by failing \(em unrecognized subcommand on stderr, exit 2 \(em
which a caller reads as "no capabilities at all". See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.TP
.B enroll
Capture and store a face embedding for authentication.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username to enroll (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-label \fIlabel\fR
Label for this face model.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-setup\-check
Go straight to the capture loop. Without it, an install whose setup\-complete
marker is missing is offered \fBfacelock setup\fR first and enrolls through it,
since setup enrolls a face itself. This is for a tree assembled by hand or by a
configuration manager, where the marker was never written but the models,
database and encryption key are in place; enrollment still fails on its own
terms if any of them is not.
.RE
.TP
.B remove \fImodel_id\fR
Remove a specific face model by its numeric ID.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B clear
Remove all face models for a user.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B list
List enrolled face models.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output as JSON.
.RE
.TP
.B test
Test face recognition against enrolled models for the current or specified user.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.RE
.TP
.B preview
Live camera preview with face detection overlay.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object per frame on stdout instead of opening the graphical
preview. This flag shipped as \fB\-\-text\-only\fR, which survives as a
hidden alias and keeps parsing; the per-frame payload is unchanged.
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
User to match faces against (defaults to current user).
.RE
.TP
.B config \fR[\fIsubcommand\fR]
Show or edit configuration. With no subcommand, shows.
.RS
.TP
.B show
Print the config file path and contents, and whether the file parses. Reads a
0644 file and needs no root.
.TP
.B edit
Open the config file in \fB$EDITOR\fR, validate it on save, and restart the
daemon when a setting it caches at startup changed. Requires root.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Check system status including daemon connectivity, enrolled faces, camera
availability, and model integrity. Also reports whether the PAM module's
oneshot fallback could authenticate with the daemon unreachable, and whether
the target user's
.B is\-enrolled
marker agrees with the database. Requires root. A check that cannot be
performed is reported as such; the report never substitutes a guessed value.
.TP
.B devices
List available camera devices.
.TP
.B daemon \fR[\fIsubcommand\fR]
Run or restart the persistent authentication daemon. With no subcommand, runs
it \(em the form every shipped service unit invokes.
.RS
.TP
.B run
Run the daemon in the foreground. Registers on the D-Bus system bus as
\fBorg.facelock.Daemon\fR to service authentication and enrollment requests
from the PAM module and CLI.
.TP
.B restart
Restart the facelock daemon via systemd, or via a D-Bus shutdown request when
systemd is unavailable, in which case the daemon restarts on next use through
D-Bus activation. Requires root.
.RE
.IP
Takes \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR from GLOBAL OPTIONS above.
.TP
.B auth
One-shot authentication (used by the PAM module). Exits with code 0 on
success or non-zero on failure. The PAM module spawns
\fBfacelock auth \-\-user \fIname\fB \-\-config \fIpath\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username to authenticate. Required here and only here: the caller names the
subject, and it must never fall back to the process owner.
.RE
.IP
Takes \fB\-\-config\fR from GLOBAL OPTIONS above.
.TP
.B bench \fIsubcommand\fR
Benchmark and calibration tools.
.RS
.TP
.B cold\-auth
Measure cold auth latency (model load + first auth).
.TP
.B warm\-auth
Measure warm auth latency (models already loaded).
.TP
.B preview
Measure preview frame capture latency.
.TP
.B enrollment
Measure enrollment time.
.TP
.B model\-load
Measure ONNX model load time.
.TP
.B calibrate
Sweep thresholds and measure FAR/FRR.
.TP
.B camera\-reopen \fR[\fB\-\-iterations \fIn\fR]
Measure what reopening the camera costs, reporting the open / STREAMON / warmup
split over \fIn\fR close\(->open\(->first\-usable\-frame cycles (default 5) as a
per\-phase median. Needs no enrolled face and loads no models. This is the total
.B device.camera_release_secs
trades LED\-on time against.
.TP
.B report
Generate a comprehensive benchmark report.
.RE
.TP
.B tpm \fIsubcommand\fR
Manage the embedding encryption key and the TPM that can seal it. The group
owns the key's whole lifecycle, so \fBencrypt\fR, \fBdecrypt\fR and
\fBreseal\fR live here \(em the cipher is software AES-256-GCM either way, and
\fBencryption.method\fR decides only where the key lives.
.RS
.TP
.B status
Report TPM availability and configuration.
.TP
.B seal\-key
Seal the AES encryption key with TPM (migrate keyfile to TPM).
.TP
.B unseal\-key
Unseal the AES key from TPM back to a plaintext keyfile (migrate TPM to keyfile).
.TP
.B unseal\-check
Read-only check that the sealed AES key currently unseals, verifying the PCR
policy is still satisfied. Writes nothing and exits non-zero if the unseal
fails, which is the signal to run
.BR "facelock tpm reseal" .
.TP
.B pcr\-baseline
Display current PCR values for configured indices.
.TP
.B encrypt
Encrypt all unencrypted embeddings in the database with AES-256-GCM.
\fB\-\-generate\-key\fR generates a new encryption key without encrypting
embeddings.
.TP
.B decrypt
Decrypt all software-encrypted embeddings in the database.
.TP
.B reseal
Re-seal the TPM AES key under the current PCR values \(em the recovery step
after a firmware or kernel change moves a measured PCR and the sealed key stops
unsealing. Requires root, and applies only when \fBencryption.method\fR is
\fBtpm\fR; under any other method it errors rather than quietly doing nothing.
It prefers unsealing the existing blob, which still works while the PCR policy
is satisfied, so it is safe to run proactively before a firmware update; once
the PCRs have moved it falls back to the plaintext key backup. Use
.B "facelock tpm unseal\-check"
to find out which of those applies.
.RE
.TP
.B pam \fIsubcommand\fR
Manage the facelock line in
.I /etc/pam.d
service files. This command owns every write to that directory;
\fBsetup \-\-pam\fR is an alias onto it. \fB\-\-service\fR is repeatable on
all three verbs and defaults to \fBsudo\fR, so several services are configured
in one process under one root check. A name is looked up in
.I /etc/pam.d
first and
.I /usr/lib/pam.d
second \(em Linux\-PAM's own order, first hit wins \(em since packages ship
their configuration in the vendor directory; on current Arch \fBpolkit\fR
installs
.I /usr/lib/pam.d/polkit\-1
and no
.I /etc/pam.d
copy. Only
.I /etc/pam.d
is ever written to: a service found only in a vendor directory is copied there
first, with the facelock line already in it and a header naming what it was
forked from, and the package's file is left untouched. Configurable with
\fB[pam] config_dirs\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B add
Add the facelock line to one or more service files. Root. Accepts
\fB\-\-service \fIname\fR (repeatable), \fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
(alias \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR) to skip the per-file confirmation,
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR to also permit \fBcommon\-auth\fR,
\fBlogin\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\-ac\fR,
\fBsshd\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\-ac\fR and
\fBsystem\-login\fR, \fB\-\-if\-present\fR to treat a missing
service file as success, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to print the resolved plan
without writing, and \fB\-\-json\fR (which implies \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR,
since the prompt would block the pipeline reading the document).
\fB\-\-yes\fR never implies
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR: they are separate authorizations. Every service
is validated before any file is written. A service file that is a symlink out
of
.I /etc/pam.d
is refused rather than written through; a link that stays inside the directory
is followed, the gate is applied to the file it reaches, and the backup is
written beside the real file. A file with more than one hard link is refused
too \(em a link count says another name for it exists and not where.
.TP
.B remove
Remove the facelock line. Root. Same flags as \fBadd\fR except
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR, which it does not offer \(em removal can only
take away a way to authenticate, so there is nothing to gate. It never prompts,
and the \fI.facelock\-backup\fR file written by \fBadd\fR is left in place.
.TP
.B status
Report whether services carry the line. Reads only and needs
.B no
root: this is the probe to branch on instead of grepping
.I /etc/pam.d
yourself. Offers \fB\-\-service\fR, \fB\-\-if\-present\fR and
\fB\-\-json\fR, and neither
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR nor \fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR \(em there is no write
to preview or gate. \fB\-\-if\-present\fR means what it means on
\fBadd\fR and \fBremove\fR: an absent service file is reported and no longer
forces exit 2. The exit status is the answer; see
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.RE
.TP
.B hyprlock \fIsubcommand\fR
Manage hyprlock lock-screen integration: the face glyph in
\fBplaceholder_text\fR and the \fBignore_empty_input = false\fR setting that
lets a bare Enter submit to PAM. Runs as your normal user and refuses to run as
root, since it edits
.I ~/.config/hypr/hyprlock.conf
and root would leave root-owned files under
.IR $HOME .
A backup is taken before the first edit. Wiring
.I /etc/pam.d/hyprlock
itself is a separate, root step \(em see \fBpam add\fR above.
.RS
.TP
.B enable
Add the face icon and set \fBignore_empty_input = false\fR. \fB\-\-no\-icon\fR
sets only the functional flag, for a hyprlock font with no Nerd Font glyphs, and
leaves any existing icon alone.
.TP
.B disable
Remove the face icon, and restore \fBignore_empty_input\fR only when no
fingerprint icon coexists, so a machine using both keeps working.
.TP
.B status
Show the current hyprlock integration state.
.RE
.TP
.B audit
View the structured audit log.
.RS
.TP
.B \-f\fR, \fB\-\-follow
Follow mode: watch for new log entries.
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-lines \fIcount\fR
Number of recent entries to show (default: 20).
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Controls log verbosity via the tracing/env_filter crate. Example:
.BR RUST_LOG=debug .
.TP
.B FACELOCK_CONFIG
Override path to the configuration file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/facelock/config.toml
Main configuration file. Controls camera device, recognition thresholds,
daemon settings, security policy, and TPM options.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/
State directory. Permissions should be 710, owned by root:facelock:
traverse-only for the
.B facelock
group, nothing for anyone else. A group member can open a path it knows by
name \(em its own enrollment marker, a model file \(em but cannot list the
directory, and users outside the group cannot reach anything below it. The
group is a D-Bus access grant, not a file-read grant.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/facelock.db
SQLite database storing face embeddings. Permissions should be 600, owned
by root:root: the daemon reads it as root, and
.B facelock
group members request authentication through the daemon rather than reading
templates.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/models/
Directory containing ONNX model files (SCRFD and ArcFace). SHA256-verified
at load time. Public downloads, so permissions are 755 root:root with the
model files themselves 644; the 710 state directory above is what keeps
non-group users away. Overridable via
.I daemon.model_dir
in the configuration file; an explicitly configured directory is honored
verbatim and is never rewritten.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/
Enrollment markers read by
.BR "facelock is\-enrolled" .
Permissions should be 710, owned by root:facelock: group-traversable to a
known filename but not listable. The ownership is part of the contract \(em
the group-execute bit only works because the
.B facelock
group owns the directory.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/<user>
One user's marker, 600 and owned by that user. Contains the model count and
the time it last changed. A hint for
.BR "facelock is\-enrolled" ,
never consulted during authentication.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/org.facelock.Daemon.conf
D-Bus system bus policy file. Enforces deny-all default, allowing access only
to root and members of the \fBfacelock\fR group.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system-services/org.facelock.Daemon.service
D-Bus activation file for the facelock daemon service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_facelock.so
PAM module shared library.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (authentication passed, command completed).
.TP
.B non\-zero
Failure (authentication failed, error occurred).
.PP
.B facelock is\-enrolled
defines its exit status as its interface, so integrations branch on the status
rather than parsing stdout:
.TP
.B 0
The user has a usable enrollment.
.TP
.B 1
Not enrolled, or the enrollment is not usable. A missing marker and an
unreadable one are both reported this way.
.TP
.B 2
Error: an invalid
.BR \-\-user ,
or a marker that exists but cannot be parsed.
.PP
.B facelock pam status
uses the same 0/1/2 scale, for the same reason \(em it is a boolean query whose
exit status is the answer. Across several services the worst outcome wins:
.TP
.B 0
Every requested service carries the facelock line.
.TP
.B 1
At least one requested service exists without it.
.TP
.B 2
At least one is absent, unreadable, misnamed, hard\-linked, or symlinked out of
.IR /etc/pam.d .
With \fB\-\-if\-present\fR an absent service file no longer forces this, and
the status is decided by the services that do exist \(em absence only, so a
dangling or looping symlink is still 2.
.PP
.B facelock capabilities
always exits 0 \(em it has no failure mode. A build too old to have the command
exits 2 with clap's unrecognized-subcommand error on stderr and nothing on
stdout, which a caller reads as "no capabilities at all".
.PP
.B facelock auth
exits 0 when the face matched, 1 when it did not, and 2 on error.
.SH SECURITY
The default configuration requires an IR camera
.RB ( security.require_ir=true ).
Frame variance checks are performed during authentication to prevent
photo-based spoofing. Rate limiting is enforced by the daemon (5 attempts
per user per 60 seconds by default). IPC uses the D-Bus system bus; the
daemon verifies caller UID via
.B GetConnectionUnixUser
before executing any method, and the system bus policy in
.I org.facelock.Daemon.conf
applies a deny-all default, restricting access to root and the
.B facelock
group. Message size limits are enforced by the D-Bus daemon.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_facelock (8),
.BR pam (8),
.BR sudo (8)
